Vote counts must be read with timestamp exposure. Older replies had more time to accumulate downvotes. The pattern is still notable, but not because one specific reply was punished more. The useful observation is that conciliatory restatements, workflow explanations, and AI-use admissions all attracted negative reception, while authenticity tests and dismissal comments attracted approval.

After the methodological issue was restated in terms of transcript preservation and research transparency, replies shifted toward the poster’s wording, tone, AI-mediated writing style, and legitimacy. Comments that marked the post as confusing, AI-like, incoherent, or socially improper received more approval than comments restating the underlying methods issue. This suggests that AI-use stigma can redirect a research-methods claim into authenticity testing and boundary enforcement.

In r/sociology, AI-marked posts appeared to encounter rapid informal suppression through downvoting before sustained discussion could form. Formal moderation then operated as a later removal layer. This suggests a two-stage boundary system: crowd-level topic rejection followed by moderator-level cleanup. The case is notable because the forum’s subject area includes concepts such as othering, stigma, boundary work, and reflexivity, yet those concepts did not visibly slow the forum’s own sorting behaviour.

AI-use stigma did not simply reject the post. It changed the category of the speaker. After that, the interaction shifted from methodological discussion toward authenticity testing, legitimacy challenge, downvoting, and off-thread circulation.

